-- title:
--   Little's law for the transit queues, per queue and in total
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a dynamic pricing policy and $\pi$ a stationary distribution of its chain, and write $1/\mu_{ij}$ for the mean travel time of the pair $(i,j)$ and $\tilde q_{ij}=\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)q_{ij}(x)$. Then:
--
--   1. for every pair $(i,j)$, the expected number of vehicles in the transit queue $(i,j)$ (summed over its stages) equals $\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\Lambda_{ij}q_{ij}(x)/\mu_{ij}$;
--   2. the expected number of vehicles in transit equals
--   $$
--   \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal A}\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\frac{\Lambda_{ij}q_{ij}(x)}{\mu_{ij}};
--   $$
--   3. this quantity equals $\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal A}\Lambda_{ij}\tilde q_{ij}/\mu_{ij}$;
--   4. it is at most $K$.
--
--   This is Little's law applied to the transit queues; together with flow balance it shows that $\tilde q$ is feasible for Problem (2) when $\Delta\ge K$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - generalization: the optimal policy q* and its steady-state probability π* → any dynamic policy q and any stationary distribution π of its chain; reason: the argument uses only stationarity; q* and π* are a special case
--   - generalization: the expected number of vehicles traveling, summed over the pairs → also the same identity for each transit queue (i,j) separately; reason: the per-pair identity implies the paper's sum
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → no connectivity assumption on the demand graph; reason: no step uses it; strongly connected instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, proof of Proposition 1, p. 7 (unnumbered displays)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_workload

namespace VehShare.SeqBounds

/-- **Little's law for the transit queues.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), proof of Proposition 1, p. 7 (unnumbered displays).

For every dynamic policy `q` and every stationary distribution `π`:
1. for every pair `(i, j)`, the expected number of vehicles in the transit queue `(i, j)` (all its
   stages) equals `∑_x π(x) Λ_ij q_ij(x)/μ_ij`;
2. summed over the pairs, `∑_{(i,j)} ∑_x π(x) Λ_ij q_ij(x)/μ_ij` equals the expected number of
   vehicles in transit;
3. it equals the workload `∑_{(i,j)} Λ_ij q̃_ij/μ_ij` of `q̃_ij = ∑_x π(x) q_ij(x)`;
4. it is at most `K`.

**Formalization Note.** Stated for every policy (the paper's optimal policy is a special case),
and per transit queue as well as summed (clause 1 is a generalization; clause 2 is the paper's
display). `1/μ_ij` is `meanTime P i j`. -/
theorem littles_law_transit (P : Instance) (K : ℕ) (q : Policy P K) (π : State P K → ℝ)
    (hπ : IsStationary q π) :
    (∀ i j : P.L, ∑ z, π z * (P.Λ i j * q.1 z i j * meanTime P i j) =
        ∑ z, π z * ∑ s, (z.1 (Sum.inr ⟨(i, j), s⟩) : ℝ)) ∧
      ∑ i, ∑ j, ∑ z, π z * (P.Λ i j * q.1 z i j * meanTime P i j) =
        ∑ z, π z * ∑ p : (Σ p : P.L × P.L, P.Ph p.1 p.2), (z.1 (Sum.inr p) : ℝ) ∧
      ∑ i, ∑ j, ∑ z, π z * (P.Λ i j * q.1 z i j * meanTime P i j) =
        workload P (fun i j => ∑ z, π z * q.1 z i j) ∧
      workload P (fun i j => ∑ z, π z * q.1 z i j) ≤ K := by sorry

end VehShare.SeqBounds
